  (text "J1 -- 94-way ECU harness connector" (at 261.0 130.0 0)
    (effects (font (size 2.0 2.0)) (justify left))
    (uuid 61d7555c-09d7-4a52-986b-ed7c7ac27bf3)
  )
  (text "41 pins wired, 53 left deliberately empty" (at 261.0 136.0 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.6 1.6)) (justify left))
    (uuid f1f3e067-b034-4345-ba09-ce8304a55761)
  )
  (text "THE POWER GROUND HAS NO PIN, AND THAT IS THIS SHEET'S FINDING.\n\nGP3.8703.C4.pdf shows pin 21 as the battery positive feed and\nshows no battery negative anywhere on the ECU connector. Its own\nscope note explains why -- it is a field troubleshooting diagram\nand it omits \"power grounds, unused pins and internal-only\npins\" -- so this is a gap in the SOURCE, not a contradiction in\nit.\n\nIt is still a gap that stops a board. Every other sheet in this\nproject returns to GND: the injector low sides carry 18 A into\nit, the metering unit up to 1.35 A, and the analog front ends\nreference their zeners and their 22 nF filters to it. At this\nboundary that net has nowhere to go.\n\nIt cannot be solved by choosing. Fifty pins have no established\nfunction and several of them are certainly grounds -- a 94-way\nconnector feeding an 18 A injector stage does not have one\nreturn -- but picking which is exactly the guess this project\nforbids, and a wrong ground pin is not a rework, it is a\nharness that has to be remade.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY SETTLES IT: the connector part number and the OEM\npin list, or continuity measurement on the machine between the\nconnector shell and battery negative. The reference names the\nfirst as the missing document. The second needs the machine and\nbelongs with U16/U2/U8/U14/U5." (at 14.0 130.0 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.6 1.6)) (justify left))
    (uuid a3b5f927-4b6c-491e-831b-b2b1fe6ec032)
  )
  (text "PINS SHOWN ON THE DIAGRAM AND STILL NOT WIRED\n\n  09  SYNCHRONIZATION GROUND. Drawn RED on a diagram whose own\n      legend makes red a positive feed, so the name and the\n      colour disagree. U6 on the unknowns register.\n  01  G_G_B AT1.  02  G_G_B AT2. Memo 03 suspects these are\n      power grounds rather than switch-return legs. If that is\n      right they are part of the answer to the note on the left.\n      No MCU pin is assigned either way, because guessing which\n      would violate the same rule.\n\nNETS THAT EXIST AND HAVE NO PIN YET\n\n  TRIP_LOOP     the trip-module supervised loop is NEW -- it is\n                not on the OEM diagram at all, so it needs one\n                of the 50 unconfirmed pins. discrete_io draws\n                the front end; the pin waits on the same\n                document the ground does.\n  EGR_HIGH_59   wired here, because pins 59 and 81 ARE on the\n  EGR_LOW_81    diagram -- but the bridge that drives them is\n                the blocked half of metering_egr, waiting on a\n                gate driver rated above the 73.3 V this board's\n                own battery rail reaches on ISO 7637-2 pulse 2a.\n                The pins are real; the driver is not chosen.\n\nWHY PINS 34 AND 36 ARE SIGNALS AND PIN 08 IS GROUND\n\n  34 and 36 are SHARED sensor returns -- 34 for boost pressure\n  and coolant temperature, 36 for EGR position and oil\n  pressure. A shared return carries both sensors' current, so\n  its own resistance is an offset on both readings, which is\n  why sensors_analog carries them into difference amplifiers\n  rather than to ground. 08 is rail pressure's DEDICATED\n  return: nothing to reject, so it is simply ground here." (at 400.0 130.0 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.6 1.6)) (justify left))
    (uuid 5eee38cc-d8bb-404e-8f09-1ca7a0eaa792)
  )
  (text "NO PART NUMBER, SO NO FOOTPRINT. The reference records a 94-pin connector from GP3.8703.C4.pdf page 4 and nothing further -- no Bosch part number, no keying, no mating half. CONN_ECU_94 therefore carries pin COUNT and pin NUMBERS, which are facts read off a document, and\nno footprint, which would be a guess about a physical interface that has to mate with a harness already on a machine. Pin NAMES on the symbol are the diagram's own signal names where it shows the pin, and KiCad's blank elsewhere. This is the same treatment the op-amps on\nsensors_analog and the comparator on speed_inputs get, for the same reason: the symbol asserts what is known and declines to assert what is not." (at 14.0 390.0 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.6 1.6)) (justify left))
    (uuid 03cd641f-7dc0-4f9a-9c72-3782e9a3de23)
  )
